WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Superluminous supernovae reach unusually high intrinsic luminosities. Their

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.160
light curves and spectra vary, so brightness alone

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.980
does not identify one engine. On the absolute

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.920
magnitude scale, more negative means brighter in the

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.280
same band. Apparent brightness also depends on distance

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.900
and cannot establish this intrinsic category by itself.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.590
In a hot, massive core, energetic photons can

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.270
create electron and positron pairs. That change softens

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.590
pressure support. Gravity drives contraction, which heats the

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.190
core and accelerates advanced burning. In the full

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.510
disruption regime, explosive burning can unbind the star.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.690
Follow the order: support weakens, contraction heats the

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.130
core, then thermonuclear burning responds.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.900
An initial stellar mass is not the same

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.460
as a final carbon and oxygen core mass.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.840
Material outside the core, mass loss and composition

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.100
affect the evolution. The lecture’s rough mass scales

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.080
are model guideposts, not a guaranteed fate for

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.520
every heavier star. Extreme luminosity alone does not

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.840
prove a measured core mass or a pair

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.220
instability explosion.
